/* ---- recap ---- */
{s:RC,k:'recap',term:'Recap',say:'Recap in three. RMS is the heating value: peak over root two for a sine. Form factor one point one one and peak factor one point four one four hold for a sine only. And phasors add tip to tail.',cues:{r1:'RMS is the',r2:'Form factor one',r3:'And phasors add'},panels:[["RMS value","BLU",'ctRms',['Heats like DC:','Vrms = Vₘ/√2 = 0.707 Vₘ'],'r1'],["Two ratios","ORG",'ctRatio',['FF = 1.11, PF = 1.414','for a pure sine only'],'r2'],["Phasors","GRN",'ctTip',['Add tip to tail:','20∠0° + 15∠60° = 30.4∠25.3°'],'r3']],live:[0,1,2]},
/* ---- exam practice: the book's own questions and model answers ---- */
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why does multiplying a phasor by j rotate it by 90°?",say:'Question one. Why does multiplying a phasor by jay rotate it by ninety degrees?',cues:{k:'Question one',q:'Why does multiplying'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why does multiplying a phasor by j rotate it by 90°?",say:'In polar form, jay is one at ninety degrees. Multiplying multiplies the lengths and adds the angles. So the length stays, and the angle grows by ninety.',cues:{a1:'In polar form',a2:'Multiplying multiplies the',a3:'So the length'},al:[["j = 1∠90°",'a1'],["Multiply lengths, add angles: r∠θ × 1∠90° = r∠(θ + 90°)",'a2'],["Same length, angle + 90°",'a3']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),CT_L(40,300,580,300,CT_AX,2)+CT_L(300,40,300,460,CT_AX,2)+`<circle cx="300" cy="300" r="90" fill="none" stroke="${CT_AX}" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 6"/>`+CT_ph(300,300,90,0,CT_MUT,3)+CT_ph(300,300,90,90,ORG,4)+CT_T(400,330,'1',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:CT_MUT})+CT_T(312,200,'j = 1∠90°',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:ORG}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),CT_ph(300,300,220,25,BLU,5)+CT_T(470,186,'r∠θ',{fs:26,fw:800,fill:BLU}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a3'),CT_ph(300,300,220,115,GRN,5)+CT_arc(300,300,130,25,115,ORG,3)+CT_T(70,96,'r∠(θ + 90°)',{fs:26,fw:800,fill:GRN})+CT_T(330,150,'+90°',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:ORG}))}`},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"A sine has Vm = 100 V. Find Vrms, the half-cycle average and the form factor.",say:'Question two. A sine has a peak of one hundred volts. Find the RMS value, the half-cycle average, and the form factor.',cues:{k:'Question two',q:'A sine has'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"A sine has Vm = 100 V. Find Vrms, the half-cycle average and the form factor.",say:'The RMS value is one hundred over root two: seventy point seven volts. The half-cycle average is two hundred over pi: sixty-three point seven volts. So the form factor is one point one one.',cues:{fk:'The RMS value',k1:'The RMS value',k1b:'seventy point seven volts',k2:'The half-cycle',k2b:'sixty-three point seven volts',k3:'So the form',k3b:'is one point'},calc:{f:[`${CT_sub('V','rms')} = Vₘ/√2`,`${CT_sub('V','avg')} = 2Vₘ/π,  FF = ${CT_sub('V','rms')}/${CT_sub('V','avg')}`],hint:'Vₘ = 100 V, a pure sine.',fk:'fk',steps:[["RMS value",[`${CT_sub('V','rms')} = 100/√2`,`${CT_sub('V','rms')} = 70.7 V`],["k1","k1b"]],["Half-cycle average",[`${CT_sub('V','avg')} = 2 × 100/π`,`${CT_sub('V','avg')} = 63.7 V`],["k2","k2b"]],["Form factor",["FF = 70.7/63.7","FF = 1.11"],["k3","k3b"]]],live:[0,1,2]}},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"A cheap AC meter calibrated for a sine reads 100 V on a square wave of peak Vm. It takes the half-cycle average and multiplies by 1.11. Find Vm and the true RMS. Is the reading high or low?",say:'Question three. A cheap meter, calibrated for a sine, reads one hundred volts on a square wave. It scales the half-cycle average by one point one one. Find the peak and the true RMS. Is the reading high or low?',cues:{k:'Question three',q:'A cheap meter'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"A cheap AC meter calibrated for a sine reads 100 V on a square wave of peak Vm. It takes the half-cycle average and multiplies by 1.11. Find Vm and the true RMS. Is the reading high or low?",say:'A square wave sits at its peak, so its half-cycle average is the peak. One point one one times the peak is one hundred, so the peak is ninety point one volts. Its true RMS is also the peak. So it reads high, by about eleven percent.',cues:{a0:'A square wave',a1:'One point one',a2:'Its true RMS',a3:'So it reads'},al:[["1.11 Vₘ = 100 V, so Vₘ = 90.1 V",'a1'],["Square wave: true RMS = Vₘ = 90.1 V",'a2'],["So 100 V reads about 11% high",'a3']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${g('pop',tk(c,'a0'),CT_T(30,40,'square wave: avg = RMS = Vₘ',{fs:24,fw:800})+CT_L(30,150,580,150,CT_AX,2)+CT_sq(30,520,150,66)+CT_T(586,92,'+Vₘ',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'end',fill:BLU})+CT_T(586,226,'−Vₘ',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'end',fill:BLU}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),CT_T(30,276,'Vₘ = 100 / 1.11 = 90.1 V',{fs:26,fw:800,fill:BLU}))}
${g('growX',tk(c,'a2'),`<rect x="30" y="300" width="${(400*90.1/100).toFixed(1)}" height="34" rx="6" fill="${GRN}"/>`+CT_T(30+400*0.901+10,326,'true RMS 90.1 V',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:GRN}))}
${g('growX',tk(c,'a3'),`<rect x="30" y="350" width="400" height="34" rx="6" fill="${ORG}"/>`+CT_T(440,376,'meter 100 V',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:ORG})+CT_T(30,424,'reads about 11% high',{fs:26,fw:800,fill:ORG}))}`},
{s:LS,k:'figs',term:'Your notes',title:'For your notes',say:'Your free notes are the PDF linked in the description. Redraw the supply wave with its two level lines, and the phasor sum, from memory.',cues:{t:'Your free notes',f:'Redraw the supply'},tags:[["Free notes for this lesson (PDF)", "link in the description"], ["The two chapters", "Sinusoids: RMS and average values, form factor; Phasors and phasor diagrams"], ["Key terms and glossary", "at the end of the notes"]],figs:[["One cycle of the supply", "325 V peak, 229.8 V RMS, 206.9 V half-cycle average; one period of 20 ms, so 50 Hz."], ["Adding phasors tip to tail", "20∠0° + 15∠60° = 30.4∠25.3° V: the diagonal of the parallelogram, not 35 V."]],note:"Sources your free notes draw on: William H. Hayt, Jack E. Kemmerly and Steven M. Durbin (Engineering Circuit Analysis); Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku (Fundamentals of Electric Circuits); M. E. Van Valkenburg (Network Analysis)"},
{s:OUT,k:'outro',term:'Outro',say:'So remember: the socket\'s two hundred thirty volts is RMS, the sine\'s factors fit only the sine, and phasors add as arrows. Next time, impedance and admittance. See you there!',cues:{f:'So remember',w1:'the socket\'s',w2:'the sine\'s factors',w3:'and phasors add',n:'Next time',s:'See you there'},words:[['230 V IS RMS','BLU','w1'],['1.11 AND 1.414: SINE ONLY','ORG','w2'],['PHASORS ADD AS ARROWS','GRN','w3']],next:["NEXT TIME", "Next lesson", "Impedance and admittance", "book"],end:'See you there!'}
